(function () {
  function P(q, a, h, s) { return { q: q, a: a, h: h || "", s: s || "" }; }

  var SSC_JE_ENC3_TOMFLUIDS = {};

  SSC_JE_ENC3_TOMFLUIDS.tom = [
    P("The maximum recommended belt speed for a flat leather belt drive on a standard cast-iron pulley is approximately ___ m/s.", "20–25", "Remember industrial belt speed limits for standard pulleys.", "Leather belts on cast-iron pulleys are limited to about 20–25 m/s. Higher speeds cause excessive heat, slip, and wear. V-belts allow up to about 30 m/s; fabric belts up to 35 m/s."),
    P("Which one of the following statements is CORRECT about a Porter governor?", "As the spindle speed increases, the balls fly outward and the sleeve rises.", "Centrifugal force of the balls balances the central load.", "At higher speed the centrifugal force exceeds the weight, so the balls move outward, lifting the sleeve. The height relation is h = 895/N² for a simple pendulum governor."),
    P("A flywheel regulates the speed fluctuation within a cycle, while a governor controls the ___ .", "mean speed", "One is an energy-storage device, the other a speed regulator.", "The flywheel absorbs energy when the turning moment is above the mean and returns it when below, limiting cyclic fluctuation. The governor adjusts the fuel/working fluid supply to hold the mean speed."),
    P("Which one of the following statements about standard full-depth involute teeth is INCORRECT?", "The addendum is equal to 0.6 times the module.", "Recall the addendum, dedendum and clearance proportions.", "Standard full-depth proportions: addendum = 1.0m, dedendum = 1.25m, working depth = 2.0m, tip clearance = 0.25m. The addendum is therefore m, not 0.6m."),
    P("A standard spur gear has module 5 mm and a pitch circle diameter of 200 mm. The number of teeth is ___ .", "40", "Relate pitch diameter D, module m and teeth Z.", "Z = D/m = 200/5 = 40 teeth. The circular pitch is pc = πm and the pitch diameter is D = mZ."),
    P("The minimum number of teeth without undercutting for a standard 20° full-depth pinion is ___ .", "18", "Interference depends on the pressure angle.", "For 20° full-depth involute action the limiting value is 17 to 18 teeth; the standard design rule uses 18 teeth for a 20° pressure angle and 32 teeth for 14.5°."),
    P("The circular pitch of a gear having module 4 mm is ___ mm.", "4π ≈ 12.57", "Circular pitch is pitch-circle arc distance between corresponding points.", "pc = πm = π × 4 = 12.566 mm. Note also the diametral pitch pd = π/pc and module m = 25.4/pd for inch series."),
    P("The clearance between the root circle of one gear and the addendum circle of the meshing gear is ___ for standard teeth.", "0.25m where m is the module", "Clearance is the gap between addendum and dedendum.", "Tip clearance = dedendum − addendum = 1.25m − 1.0m = 0.25m. This space prevents interference and holds oil film."),
    P("Which one of the following statements is CORRECT?", "An isochronous governor maintains a constant speed for all positions of the sleeve.", "Isochronous means a zero range of speed.", "In an isochronous governor the height remains the same at every speed, i.e. the range of speed is zero. It is theoretically ideal but practically gives hunting."),
    P("The gyroscopic couple acting on the rotor of a turbine during ship turning is C = Iωωp, where I is the ___ .", "moment of inertia of the rotor about the spin axis", "The couple uses the rotor inertia about its own spin axis.", "C = Iωωp, with I = polar moment of inertia of the rotor, ω = spin velocity and ωp = precession velocity. For marine and aero applications the precession is given by the turn or pitch rate."),
    P("In a simple gear train with an idler gear between the driver and driven gear, the velocity ratio ___ .", "is unchanged in magnitude but the direction is reversed", "The idler only reverses rotation.", "The idler cancels out of the ratio; only the driver and driven teeth matter for magnitude. Direction is reversed once for each external mesh."),
    P("For a helical gear the axial thrust is expressed as Ft tan β, where β is the ___ .", "helix angle", "The helix angle produces the axial component.", "Fa = Ft tan β. To eliminate axial load in heavy drives, double-helical (herringbone) gears use opposite-handed halves so the thrust components cancel."),
    P("The turning moment diagram of a single-cylinder four-stroke engine has ___ cycle(s) for two revolutions of the crankshaft.", "one", "Power is delivered once every 720° of crank rotation.", "A four-stroke engine produces one positive loop per working cycle (two crank revolutions). The balancing energy is stored in the flywheel during the three non-power strokes."),
    P("The natural circular frequency of a mass–spring system of stiffness k and mass m is ___ rad/s.", "√(k/m)", "Standard undamped natural frequency formula.", "ωn = √(k/m). In Hz, fn = (1/2π)√(k/m). Time period T = 2π√(m/k). The frequency is independent of initial amplitude for a linear spring."),
    P("At the critical speed of a shaft, ___ .", "the whirling frequency equals the natural frequency of transverse vibration", "Critical speed is the resonance condition.", "When shaft rotational speed equals its lateral natural frequency the shaft whirls with large amplitude. Operating near this speed is dangerous; shafts are stiffer-than or softer-than operating ranges."),
    P("For a single-plate clutch using the uniform-pressure assumption, the torque T is given by μF × R, where the equivalent radius R is ___ .", "2(R1³ − R2³) / 3(R1² − R2²)", "Uniform pressure uses volume-moment mean radius.", "Uniform pressure (new clutch): R = 2(R1³−R2³)/3(R1²−R2²). Uniform wear (worn clutch): R = (R1+R2)/2. The uniform-wear radius is smaller, giving lower torque."),
    P("The number of degrees of freedom of a rigid lamina moving in a plane is ___ .", "3", "Two translations and one rotation.", "A planar rigid body has three independent motions: dx, dy and rotation θ. A free rigid body in space has six degrees of freedom."),
    P("A tangent cam drives a roller follower. The base circle is the smallest circle drawn ___ .", "tangent to the cam profile with centre at the cam axis", "The base circle is the starting circle of the profile.", "The base circle radius is the minimum radius of the cam measured from the cam centre to the lowest point of the cam profile. The prime circle is drawn through the pitch point and is tangent to the pitch curve."),
    P("The chain length Lp (in pitches) joining sprockets with N1 and N2 teeth at a centre distance C (in pitches) is ___ .", "Lp = 2C + (N1 + N2)/2 + (N2 − N1)²/(4π²C)", "Standard chain-length formula for two sprockets.", "Lp = 2C + (N1+N2)/2 + (N2−N1)²/(4π²C). Nearly identical sprockets simplify the last term to zero. The result is rounded up to an even number of pitches."),
    P("For a band brake, the ratio of tight to slack tensions is T1/T2 = ___ .", "e^(μθ)", "Friction on a drum obeys the belt equation.", "T1/T2 = e^(μθ), θ in radians being the angle of lap. For a band of thickness t, subtract t/(drum radius) from θ to allow for bending stiffness."),
    P("Which one of the following is NOT a type of brake?", "Helical brake", "Brakes are classified by construction and application.", "Common brake types: block, band, internal expanding shoe, disc, cone, differential band. No 'helical brake' exists; the helical form is used for springs, not brakes."),
    P("In a Proell governor the balls are mounted ___ .", "on extensions of the lower arms beyond the pivots", "Distinguishes Proell from Porter geometry.", "The Proell governor carves the balls on extensions of the arms beyond the pivot pins, in contrast to the Porter governor where the central load is on the sleeve only. This arrangement affects the controlling-force lines."),
    P("For a Porter governor the relation between the height h (mm) and the speed N (rpm), ignoring friction, is h = ___ .", "895/N²", "Memorise the standard 895 constant.", "For the simple pendulum-type governor, h = 895/N² mm. Example: N = 90 rpm → h = 895/8100 ≈ 0.11 m = 110 mm. This holds when friction and sleeve load effects are neglected."),
    P("The viscous damping ratio of an underdamped single-DOF system is obtained experimentally from the ___ .", "logarithmic decrement", "Successive amplitude peaks give the decay rate.", "δ = ln(x1/x2) = 2πζ/√(1−ζ²). For light damping δ ≈ 2πζ. Damping is measured from the free-decay record of displacement peaks."),
    P("Assertion (A): The Hartnell governor is a spring-loaded governor. Reason (R): The controlling force in a Hartnell governor is provided entirely by gravity acting on central dead weights.", "A is true but R is false.", "Springs, not weights, control the Hartnell governor.", "The Hartnell governor uses a helical spring on the sleeve spindle together with bell-crank levers carrying the balls. Reason (R) wrongly describes the dead-weight-loaded Porter governor."),
    P("For maximum power transmission by a belt, the belt speed should be such that ___ .", "the centrifugal tension equals one-third of the maximum total tension", "Optimum speed maximises the effective tension product.", "Power is maximum when Tc = T/3, i.e. belt speed v = √(T/3m) where m is mass per unit length. Centrifugal tension Tc = mv²."),
    P("For a V-belt with groove angle 2β and coefficient of friction μ, the ratio T1/T2 is given by log_e(T1/T2) = ___ .", "μθ / sin β", "The wedge action raises the effective friction in V-grooves.", "For a V-belt, log_e(T1/T2) = μθ/sin β where 2β is the included groove angle (often 40°). Because sin β < 1, the V-belt develops a much higher grip than a flat belt."),
    P("Assertion (A): Adding mass to a spring–mass system lowers the natural frequency. Reason (R): Natural frequency varies as the inverse square root of mass.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "ωn = √(k/m) depends on both parameters.", "Since ωn ∝ 1/√m, an increase in mass decreases the natural frequency. The stiffness k is assumed unchanged. This confirms the assertion."),
    P("Which one of the following statements about a cam is INCORRECT?", "The pitch circle and the base circle are the same thing.", "They are distinct circles used in profile construction.", "The base circle is the minimum circle of the cam profile; the pitch circle passes through the pitch point when the follower dwells on the base circle. The prime circle is tangent to the pitch curve. The three differ."),
    P("During the working stroke of a reciprocating engine, the flywheel ___ .", "stores the excess energy of the power stroke", "Absorbs surplus torque above the mean value.", "During expansion the torque exceeds the mean, and the flywheel accelerates, absorbing energy. During the other strokes the flywheel gives back energy, smoothing out the speed fluctuation."),
    P("The coefficient of speed fluctuation Cf of a flywheel is defined as ___ .", "(N1 − N2)/Nmean", "The ratio of speed range to mean speed.", "Cf = (Nmax − Nmin)/Nmean. The required flywheel energy E = Iωmean²Cf. A smaller Cf means closer speed regulation but a heavier, costlier flywheel."),
    P("Assertion (A): A band and block brake gives a more uniform braking torque than a simple band brake. Reason (R): Each block contributes its own friction and spreads the contact over a larger angle.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Blocks help distribute contact and improve torque.", "Attaching wooden/leather blocks to the band increases contact area and alters the effective coefficient of friction, smoothing the torque. T1/T2 = (1+μ tan α/2)/(1−μ tan α/2)^n per block for n blocks."),
    P("At the dead centre positions of a reciprocating engine crank, the piston ___ .", "has zero velocity but maximum acceleration (inertia)", "The crank pin reverses direction there.", "At TDC/BDC, velocity v = 0; the acceleration is finite and maximum due to the changing direction. This is why firing occurs slightly after TDC to avoid shock."),
    P("The main purpose of a flywheel on a punching press is to ___ .", "store energy during the light portion and release it during the punch", "Punching demands a large power pulse.", "The motor drives the flywheel at uniform speed; during the brief punch blow the flywheel supplies the peak energy and slows slightly, letting a small motor handle a high instantaneous demand."),
    P("Assertion (A): The natural frequency of a shaft can be raised by increasing its diameter. Reason (R): Increasing diameter increases the lateral stiffness more than the mass.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Stiffness rises as d⁴ while mass rises as d².", "For a solid shaft, stiffness EI/L³ ∝ d⁴, while the mass ∝ d²L. Since d⁴/d² = d², the ratio ωn = √(stiffness/mass) rises with d. Hence larger diameters raise critical speed."),
    P("Which one of the following statements about belt drives is CORRECT?", "The angle of lap on the smaller pulley is always less than that on the larger pulley.", "The smaller pulley subtends less of the belt.", "For an open belt drive, the smaller pulley has the smaller angle of contact, which sets the limiting slipping condition. The power capacity is governed by e^(μθ) on the smaller pulley."),
  ];

  SSC_JE_ENC3_TOMFLUIDS.tom.push(
    P("Assertion (A): Two meshing spur gears must have the same module. Reason (R): The pitch-line velocities of the two gears are equal.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Mating gears share pitch velocity and module.", "For correct meshing, pc (hence module) must be identical, and the pitch-line speeds r1ω1 = r2ω2 are equal. The velocity ratio equals the inverse tooth ratio."),
    P("The velocity ratio of an open belt drive with pulley diameters D1 (larger) and D2 (smaller), belt thickness t, neglecting slip, is ___ .", "(D1 + t)/(D2 + t)", "Effective pulley radii include the belt thickness.", "Angular speeds follow ω1/ω2 = (D2+t)/(D1+t); the velocity ratio is the inverse ratio (D1+t)/(D2+t). With slip fraction s on each pulley the ratio is (D1+t)(1−s)/(D2+t)(1−s′) for the respective pulleys."),
    P("The Coriolis component of acceleration acting on a slider sliding at velocity v on a link rotating at angular velocity ω is ___ .", "2ωv", "Combined translation and rotation produces Coriolis terms.", "aCor = 2ωv, directed at 90° to the sliding velocity. In a crank–slotted-lever quick-return mechanism, the acceleration of the slider block contains this 2ωv component and must be included in the diagram."),
    P("The total number of instantaneous centres for a mechanism having 6 links is ___ .", "15", "Instantaneous centres are chosen as link pairs.", "Number of ICs = n(n−1)/2 = 6×5/2 = 15. For a 4-bar chain there are 6 ICs, of which three are collinear (Kennedy's theorem) at any instant."),
    P("Which one of the following is a spring-controlled governor?", "Hartnell governor", "Spring governors replace weights with springs.", "Hartnell, Hartung and Wilson–Hartnell governors use springs. Porter and Proell governors are gravity (dead-weight) loaded. Pickering uses flat radial springs."),
    P("Assertion (A): The Watt governor is a pendulum-type governor. Reason (R): Its balls hang from a single central pivot and the sleeve stays at the pivot.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "The Watt governor is the simplest pendulum type.", "In the Watt governor two balls hang by arms from a single pivot above the spindle; there is no dead-weight load on the sleeve. Height h = 895/N² fits its geometry closely."),
    P("For the Watt governor, h (mm) at a speed of 63 rpm is approximately ___ .", "225", "Apply h = 895/N².", "h = 895/(63²) = 895/3969 ≈ 0.2255 m ≈ 225 mm. Governing height falls steeply as speed rises, calling for a spring-controlled type above about 100 rpm."),
    P("The sensitiveness of a governor is defined as ___ .", "(Nmax − Nmin)/Nmean", "It is the reciprocal of sensitiveness meaning S = 2(N1−N2)/(N1+N2) style relations.", "Sensitiveness = (N1−N2)/Nmean = 2(N1−N2)/(N1+N2). A sensitive governor yields a large sleeve lift for a small speed change."),
    P("Hunting of a governor is due to ___ .", "excessive sensitiveness", "Over-sensitive governors overshoot continuously.", "When the governor is too sensitive the sleeve overshoots the equilibrium position repeatedly, producing sustained oscillation (hunting). Proper damping and stiffness cure this."),
    P("The stability of a governor means that ___ .", "each speed corresponds to only one equilibrium radius", "There is a single sleeve position per speed.", "A stable governor returns to a fixed equilibrium height for each speed. In unstable governors the controlling-force and centrifugal-force curves intersect at more than one point at a given speed."),
    P("Assertion (A): A two-plane (dynamic) balance removes both unbalanced forces and couples. Reason (R): Two correction masses can produce an arbitrary force pair to cancel both the force and couple resultants.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Dynamic balancing requires two planes.", "A single mass can cancel pure unbalance, but a couple requires two masses in two planes. Dynamic (two-plane) balancing therefore cancels both ΣF and ΣC = 0, which is the Lanchester-style requirement for long rotors."),
    P("The Lanchester balancer cancels the ___ of an engine using two counter-rotating shafts.", "secondary harmonic force", "It is specifically tuned to the second harmonic.", "The Lanchester scheme runs an extra pair of shafts at twice crankshaft speed carrying eccentric masses generating a 2ω harmonic force that cancels the secondary unbalance of the engine."),
    P("The whirling of a shaft is most likely to occur when ___ .", "the shaft speed equals the critical speed", "Resonance between rotation and bending natural frequency.", "At the critical speed the amplitude grows to unacceptable values. Power transmission shafts run either well below (stiff shafts) or above (flexible shafts) the first critical speed."),
    P("For a ship turning in a horizontal plane, the angle between the spin axis and vertical is ___, the axis of spin being fore-and-aft. The gyroscopic couple acts about the ___ axis.", "zero; horizontal transverse axis", "Longitudinal spin yields pitching couple.", "The spin axis is longitudinal and perpendicular to the turning axis. The couple vector is transverse, tilting the ship fore-and-aft (pitch). Reverse rotation of the rotor reverses the couple."),
    P("Which one of the following statements about vibration isolation is INCORRECT?", "An isolator reduces transmitted force only when the frequency ratio r = ω/ωn is below √2.", "Below √2 the isolator amplifies the transmitted force.", "Transmissibility T1 = 1/(1−(ω/ωn)²). For r < √2 the isolation is negative (amplifies). Real isolation needs r > √2, usually r ≥ 3 by choosing soft mounts and heavy base."),
    P("The transmissibility of a vibrating system with damping becomes equal to unity when ___ .", "r = √2", "Amplification region ends at √2.", "At r = √2 with ζ = 0, TR = 1. For larger frequency ratios TR < 1 giving isolation; the maximum reduction requires soft springs and low damping."),
    P("Assertion (A): Damping reduces vibration amplitude at resonance. Reason (R): Energy dissipation per cycle converts mechanical energy to heat.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Damping absorbs energy each cycle.", "At resonance ζ ≪ 1 would give unbounded motion; viscous damping dissipates energy and caps the amplitude. Q factor = 1/(2ζ) measures the resonance sharpness."),
    P("The unbalance of a rotor measured by a single factor that combines force and couple into one number is the ___ .", "unbalance tolerance usually in g·mm (gram-millimetre)", "Balance quality grades specify a residual unbalance limit.", "Balance quality G = (e × ω)/1000 with e in mm giving the permissible residual specific unbalance. Rotors are graded G1, G2.5, G6.3 etc. per ISO 1940."),
    P("A dynamic balancing machine measures unbalance in ___ .", "two planes simultaneously using the influence-coefficient method", "Two-plane measurement separates force and couple.", "Run-up with the trial mass in two planes gives influence coefficients that predict the correction masses. This is the standard hard-bearing two-plane balancing method."),
    P("Assertion (A): A brake absorbs kinetic energy and converts it to heat. Reason (R): Braking torque times angular velocity is the braking power.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Brake power is Tω.", "Braking power = T × ω. Kinetic energy ½Iω² is dissipated as heat; clutch/brake design limits working temperature and wear rate."),
    P("For a simple band brake with μ = 0.3 and angle of lap 240° (4π/3 rad), the ratio T1/T2 is about ___ .", "3.5", "Evaluate e^(μθ).", "μθ = 0.3 × 4.1888 = 1.2566. e^1.2566 ≈ 3.51. So T1 ≈ 3.5 T2. Halving μ to 0.15 lowers the ratio to about 1.87."),
    P("Assertion (A): In a differential band brake the leverage ratio is (a/b) where a and b are distances of the pivot from the drum centre. Reason (R): The two ends of the band are attached to the lever on opposite sides of the fulcrum.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Passive and active end positions set the ratio.", "With the band ends tied to the two arms on opposite sides of the pivot, the effective effort arm difference gives a high mechanical advantage T1×a vs T2×b; signs depend on which end drives."),
    P("The prony brake measures the ___ of the shaft it grips.", "torque and hence power at a known speed", "The brake torque is read through a dynamometer.", "Torque T = net spring load × arm length. Power = Tω = 2πNT/60 with N in rpm. It is a pure absorption dynamometer for IC engines."),
    P("The Foucault (eddy current) dynamometer is often used to measure the torque of ___ .", "high-speed turbines", "No contact braking, ideal at high speed.", "Eddy-current (Foucault) brakes generate drag with no mechanical contact, so they suit very high rotational speeds and allow smooth torque control via field current."),
    P("Assertion (A): Conical (cone) clutches give larger torque for the same axial force than flat clutches. Reason (R): The normal force component is amplified by the cone angle.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Cone geometry multiplies the friction capacity.", "T = μW(1/sin α)(R1+R2)/2 for cone angles α. The 1/sin α term exceeds unity, so the same axial force creates a larger normal force on the conical surface."),
    P("For a centrifugal pump impeller the term 'specific speed' Ns is defined using ___ .", "Ns = N√Q/H^(3/4)", "The dimensionless-ish index for pumps.", "Ns = N√Q / H^(3/4) (Q in m³/s, H in m, N in rpm). Francis nuts ≈ 60–300 (metric), Kaplan ≈ 300–900, Pelton ≈ below 60, mixed-flow in between."),
    P("The specific speed of a Pelton wheel turbine (metric units) generally lies in the range ___ .", "5–35 (sometimes quoted 10–35)", "Pelton wheels are low-specific-speed impulse wheels.", "Pelton: Ns 5–35; Francis: 60–300; Kaplan/Propeller: 300–900 (metric). Higher Ns implies the machine handles more flow for less head."),
    P("Assertion (A): A higher specific speed turbine runs at a higher rpm for the same head and power. Reason (R): Ns groups geometrically similar machines to one number.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Ns is the similarity index.", "Ns = N√P / H^(5/4) for turbines. For given H, P the rpm rises with Ns. The index collapses all geometrically similar runners into one point on the design chart."),
    P("Which one of the following statements about gear-tooth terminology is CORRECT?", "The addendum is the radial distance from the pitch circle to the top of the tooth.", "Addendum/dedendum are measured from the pitch circle radially.", "Addendum: pitch circle → addendum (tooth tip) circle. Dedendum: pitch circle → root circle. Working depth = addendum + dedendum of the mating gear."),
    P("Assertion (A): A reverted gear train is a compound train whose first and last shafts are coaxial. Reason (R): The centre distances of the two gear pairs must be equal.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Coaxiality fixes the centre distance equality.", "For the reverted train, gear pairs (1,2) and (3,4) must satisfy m1(Z1+Z2)/2 = m2(Z3+Z4)/2 so that shafts align. This makes the train compact for clocks and back gears."),
    P("Which one of the following statements about the instantaneous centre method is CORRECT?", "The relative velocity of two points on a link is zero at their instantaneous centre of that link.", "A link's IC shows no relative motion between its points.", "The IC is the point about which the link rotates at that instant. This simplifies velocity diagrams; v = ωr from each IC."),
    P("Assertion (A): Kennedy's theorem about three instant centres says the three ICs of three links are collinear. Reason (R): Each link pair shares one velocity matcher that must align with the third.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Three ICs are collinear at any instant.", "For three links 1–2, 2–3, 3–1, the ICs I12, I23, I31 lie on one straight line at every instant. This is the basis of graphical linkage analysis."),
    P("The follower lift velocity of a uniform-velocity (parabolic) cam is ___ at the extreme positions.", "finite but the acceleration is infinite", "The displacement is linear in angle and changes direction sharply.", "Uniform velocity x = (β/θ)s gives v = constant turning to jump at ends, so acceleration spikes to infinity there. This makes uniform-velocity cams wear quickly; suitable for low-speed work."),
    P("Which one of the following statements is CORRECT about the cycloidal cam?", "Its follower acceleration is completely smooth with no knocks: ideal up to high speeds.", "Cycloidal motion avoid infinite accelerations.", "Cycloidal displacement gives ideally smooth acceleration and zero jerk discontinuities at high speed, so it is used on high-speed IC-engine valve cams."),
    P("The most common cause of 'freezing' (sticking) of a spring-loaded multiple-disc clutch is ___ .", "dirt and lack of oil causing high friction between discs", "Contamination and dry discs cling together.", "Discs can drag and stick when contaminated, warped, or lubricated wrongly. Lubricated multi-plate clutches avoid stick-slip grabs."),
    P("Which one of the following vibration-isolation statements is CORRECT?", "A rigid base on soft springs with a heavy isolated mass gives low transmissibility at high frequency.", "Low natural frequency of the mount yields r >> 1.", "ranged mounting: fn low and ω high ⇒ TR ≈ (1/(ω/ωn)²) small. This is the classic machine-table isolation rule."),
    P("The damping ratio ζ of a spring–damper–mass with c = 0.5 cc is ___ .", "0.5 (underdamped)", "ζ = c/cc.", "ζ = c/(2√(km)) = c/cc. 0.5 gives a decaying oscillation; at ζ = 1 (critical) the system returns fastest without overshoot."),
    P("Assertion (A): A rotor balanced statically may still vibrate dynamically. Reason (R): Static balance corrects force but not the couple produced by unequal end weights.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Static balance misses couples.", "Static balance puts Σmr = 0; the two end unbalances form a couple that remains, exciting bearing forces. Dynamic balancers fix both."),
    P("The balancing of a reciprocating engine is complicated by ___ .", "the secondary force varying as twice crank speed superposed on primary", "The mass force has harmonic series.", "F = mrω²(cos θ + cos2θ/n + ...). Balancing primary separately and secondary separately needs two sets of counter-masses, rarely both used commercially."),
    P("The primary source of unbalance in a rotating machine part is ___ .", "eccentricity of the mass centre with respect to the rotation axis", "Mass eccentricity creates a rotating force mrω².", "Any residual eccentricity e makes a revolving force m e ω². Balance by adding/removing matter opposite the heavy spot at the same radius."),
    P("Assertion (A): The 'quality grade' of a rotor balance corresponds to its vibration velocity. Reason (R): G = (e × ω) with e in mm and ω in rad/s defines the grade number.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "G balances eccentricity and speed together.", "G = eω/1000 (mm/s units), e.g. G2.5 means 2.5 mm/s at the rotor surface. Small G means tighter balance."),
    P("Which one of the following statements about the gyroscope axes is CORRECT?", "The three axes are the spin axis, the torque (precession) axis and the vertical axis.", "The gyro uses three mutually perpendicular axes.", "Spin axis, precession axis (torque axis) and the vertical axis define gyro behaviour. Precession occurs when a torque about one axis produces rotation about the perpendicular axis."),
    P("When a top spins with angular velocity ω and leans at angle θ, the precession rate of its axis is ___ .", "ωp = (m g l)/(I ω)", "Precession about the pivot balances gravity torque.", "Torque mgl sin θ = Iωωp sin θ, so ωp = mgl/(Iω) independent of the lean angle. The spin keeps the top upright as long as ω stays high."),
    P("Assertion (A): A flywheel and a governor are both speed-fluctuation devices. Reason (R): The flywheel controls the mean speed, the governor controls the cyclic variation.", "A is true but R is false.", "The two roles are exactly opposite here.", "The flywheel moderates cyclic fluctuation; the governor regulates the mean speed. Hence the reason reverses the actual functions."),
  );

  SSC_JE_ENC3_TOMFLUIDS.fluids = [
    P("Which one of the following statements about water is CORRECT?", "Water attains maximum density at 4°C, not at 0°C.", "Density anomaly of water near freezing.", "Water reaches its maximum density of about 1000 kg/m³ at 4°C. Between 4°C and 0°C it expands slightly, which is why ice floats and ponds freeze from the top down."),
    P("The mass of 1 cubic metre of water at 4°C is approximately ___ .", "1000 kg", "Standard density reference value.", "ρ = 1000 kg/m³ at 4°C. Its specific weight is ρg = 9810 N/m³. These two numbers anchor most fluid calculations."),
    P("Assertion (A): Ice floats on water. Reason (R): The molecular lattice of ice occupies more volume than the same mass of liquid water.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Ice is less dense than liquid water.", "Ice has a hexagonal open lattice with lower density (≈ 917 kg/m³) than liquid water, so its buoyancy exceeds its weight and it floats, leaving roughly 10% of its volume above water."),
    P("Kinematic viscosity ν is related to dynamic viscosity μ and density ρ by ___ .", "ν = μ/ρ", "Kinematic viscosity divides out density.", "ν = μ/ρ, with units m²/s. Dynamic viscosity μ has units Pa·s (kg/m·s). The ratio matters in the Reynolds number Re = VD/ν."),
    P("The SI unit commonly used for kinematic viscosity is ___ ; one stoke equals ___ .", "m²/s; 1 St = 10⁻⁴ m²/s", "Remember the stoke–m²/s conversion.", "1 St = 1 cm²/s = 10⁻⁴ m²/s; 1 cSt = 10⁻⁶ m²/s. Water at 20°C has ν ≈ 1 × 10⁻⁶ m²/s = about 1 cSt."),
    P("The dynamic viscosity of water at 20°C is close to ___ (SI units).", "1.0 × 10⁻³ Pa·s", "Standard value for water viscosity.", "μ_water ≈ 1.002 × 10⁻³ Pa·s at 20°C. Kinetic ν = μ/ρ ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m²/s. Both numbers are used to set Re for pipe-flow problems."),
    P("The dynamic viscosity of air at 20°C is close to ___ (SI units).", "1.81 × 10⁻⁵ Pa·s", "Air is much less viscous than water.", "μ_air ≈ 1.8 × 10⁻⁵ Pa·s, about 55 times smaller than water's value. Note that unlike liquids, the viscosity of gases increases with temperature."),
    P("Which one of the following statements is CORRECT about liquids?", "The viscosity of a liquid decreases as temperature rises.", "Viscosity–temperature behaviour of liquids and gases differ.", "In liquids, thermal agitation loosens intermolecular bonds, so viscosity falls sharply with temperature (e.g. SAE oil). In gases, viscosity rises with temperature because molecular momentum exchange dominates."),
    P("Which one of the following statements is CORRECT about gases?", "The viscosity of a gas increases with temperature.", "Kinetic theory predicts gas viscosity varies as √T approximately.", "For gases, higher temperature increases molecular interchange and hence shear stress, so μ rises. This is why bearing oil films thin at high temperature but air friction increases."),
    P("Assertion (A): A high viscosity index oil is preferred for engines. Reason (R): High viscosity index means a smaller change of viscosity with temperature.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Viscosity index grades oils by temperature stability.", "Viscosity index (VI) measures how little the viscosity changes between 40°C and 100°C test points; high-VI multigrade oils keep lubricant film across the operating range."),
    P("One standard atmosphere is equivalent to ___ .", "10.33 m of water, 760 mm of mercury, or 1.01325 bar", "Standard pressure conversion facts.", "1 atm ≈ 101.325 kPa = 10.33 m H2O = 760 mm Hg = 1.01325 bar. These conversions recur in manometer and boiler problems."),
    P("One bar equals ___ kPa, and is equivalent to about ___ m of water column.", "100 kPa; about 10.2 m", "Bar and water-column conversion.", "1 bar = 100 kPa = 10⁵ N/m². As an equivalent water head, h = 100000/9810 ≈ 10.2 m. Note 1 bar ≈ 0.987 atm, while 1 atm ≈ 1.0133 bar."),
    P("1 N is equal to ___ dyne.", "10⁵ dyne (one hundred thousand)", "CGS–SI force conversion.", "1 N = 1 kg·m/s² = 10⁵ g·cm/s² = 10⁵ dyne. Also 1 kgf ≈ 9.81 N and 1 N ≈ 0.102 kgf."),
    P("1 kW of power equals ___ kgf·m/s.", "≈ 102 kgf·m/s (101.9)", "Convert watts to kgf·m/s using 1 kgf = 9.81 N.", "1 kW = 1000 W = 1000 N·m/s; dividing by 9.81 gives 101.9 ≈ 102 kgf·m/s. This is distinct from 1 metric HP = 75 kgf·m/s ≈ 0.736 kW, and 1 mechanical HP = 76.04 kgf·m/s ≈ 0.746 kW."),
    P("The specific gravity of mercury used in manometers is ___ .", "13.6", "Hg is 13.6 times denser than water.", "ρ_mercury = 13,600 kg/m³, S.G. = 13.6. Hence a 1 mm Hg column balances a 13.6 mm water column, giving high sensitivity in U-tube manometers."),
    P("A U-tube manometer filled with mercury shows a height difference of 100 mm. The pressure applied (excluding the column effects) is ___ .", "13.34 kPa (≈ 1.36 m of water)", "Multiply the Hg column by its density and g.", "p = ρgh = 13600 × 9.81 × 0.1 = 13,342 Pa ≈ 13.34 kPa. In water column terms that is 13,342/9810 ≈ 1.36 m."),
    P("The capillary rise of water (σ = 0.073 N/m, full wetting) in a tube of 2 mm bore is about ___ .", "15 mm", "Apply h = 4σ/(ρgD), the full-wetting capillary formula.", "h = 4σ/(ρgD) = 4 × 0.073/(1000 × 9.81 × 0.002) = 0.292/19.62 ≈ 0.0149 m ≈ 15 mm. Capillary rise is inverse in tube diameter."),
    P("The capillary depression of mercury (σ = 0.484 N/m, contact angle 140°) in a 4 mm bore tube is about ___ below the reservoir level.", "2.8 mm (about 3 mm)", "Hg is depressed because cos 140° is negative.", "h = 4σ cos θ/(ρgD) = 4 × 0.484 × (−0.766)/(13600 × 9.81 × 0.004) ≈ −1.483/533.7 ≈ −0.0028 m. The minus sign means depression below the outside level."),
    P("The surface tension of water against air at ordinary temperature is about ___ N/m.", "0.073", "Memorise the standard value.", "σ_water ≈ 0.073 N/m (0.0728). This drives capillary rise, menisci, droplet and bubble pressure inside which p = 4σ/d for a droplet and 8σ/d for a bubble."),
    P("The surface tension of mercury against air is about ___ N/m.", "0.484", "Mercury's surface tension is the largest of common liquids.", "σ_Hg ≈ 0.484 N/m, roughly 6.6 times that of water. Its high σ and contact angle > 90° explain the convex meniscus and capillary depression."),
    P("The pressure due to a 1 m high column of water is ___ kPa.", "9.81", "Hydrostatic head to pressure.", "p = ρgh = 1000 × 9.81 × 1 = 9810 Pa = 9.81 kPa. Hence 10 m of water ≈ 98.1 kPa ≈ 0.968 bar."),
    P("Pascal's law states that ___ .", "pressure in a static fluid acts equally in all directions and is the same at every point at the same level", "Foundation of hydrostatics.", "In a fluid at rest the pressure at any point is isotropic and varies only with depth. This underpins the hydraulic press and the U-tube principle."),
    P("A wooden block floats in water. The buoyant force on it equals ___ .", "the weight of the block (and the weight of the displaced water)", "Floating equilibrium condition.", "For a floating body, buoyancy = weight of the body = weight of the displaced fluid. If the body were lighter the extra buoyancy pushes it up until only part remains submerged, e.g. about 90% of ice is below the surface."),
    P("The buoyant force on a solid sphere of radius 0.1 m fully submerged in water is ___ .", "41.1 N", "Fb = ρg × displaced volume.", "V = (4/3)π r³ = (4/3)π(0.1)³ = 4.189 × 10⁻³ m³. Fb = 1000 × 9.81 × 4.189 × 10⁻³ ≈ 41.1 N. The buoyancy is independent of depth of submergence."),
    P("Which one of the following statements about flotation stability is CORRECT?", "A floating body is stable when the metacentre M lies above the centre of gravity G.", "Meta-centric height criterion.", "Stable equilibrium: GM > 0 (M above G). Neutral: GM = 0; unstable: GM < 0. GM = BM ± BG, with B the centre of buoyancy."),
    P("The metacentric height of a ship is determined experimentally by the ___ experiment.", "inclining (tilting) experiment", "Standard naval-architecture measurement.", "A known weight w is moved a distance x across the deck; the pendulum deflection θ gives GM = wx/(W tan θ) ≈ wx/(Wθ) for small θ. GM is then checked against design values."),
    P("In the inclining experiment with a shift w × x and a small heel angle θ, the metacentric height is ___ .", "GM = (w · x) / (W · tan θ)", "Balance the disturbing and restoring moments.", "The heeling moment w·x is resisted by W × GM × tan θ; equating gives GM = wx/(W tan θ). Typical GM for cargo ships is 0.3–1.0 m."),
    P("In a hydraulic press, the small plunger is 50 mm in diameter and the ram 250 mm. The effort needed to hold a load of 2000 N on the ram is ___ .", "80 N", "Pressure is common; forces scale with area ratio.", "p same on both: F1/A1 = F2/A2. A1/A2 = (50/250)² = 1/25, so F1 = 2000/25 = 80 N. The press multiplies force 25 times but the ram stroke is 25 times shorter."),
    P("The centre of pressure of a plane immersed in a static liquid lies ___ .", "always below the centroid of the area", "Second moment of area exceeds the first squared.", "For a vertical/inclined plane, ycp = yc + I/(A·yc) > yc. Only for a horizontal plane (uniform pressure) do they coincide."),
    P("A vertical rectangular gate 1 m wide × 2 m high has its top edge 1 m below the free water surface. The hydrostatic force on the gate is ___ .", "≈ 39.2 kN", "F = ρg h̄ A with h̄ the centroid depth.", "h̄ = 1 + 1 = 2 m, A = 2 m². F = 1000 × 9.81 × 2 × 2 = 39,240 N ≈ 39.2 kN. Its line of action (centre of pressure) sits at hcp = 2 + (2×(2)³/12)/(2×2)... ycp = 2 + I/(A·2) with I about centroid."),
    P("The pressure at any point in a static fluid is ___ as long as the depth is the same.", "equal in every direction", "Isotropy of hydrostatic pressure.", "At one point, pressure is direction-independent; equal-depth points have equal pressure. Only the vertical gradient ρg exists in a stationary homogeneous fluid."),
    P("The Reynolds number for flow through a pipe is defined as ___ .", "Re = ρVD/μ = VD/ν", "The dimensionless viscous-flow ratio.", "Re = ρVD/μ = VD/ν, with V mean velocity and D pipe diameter. It characterises laminar vs turbulent behaviour and similar flows."),
    P("For flow through a circular pipe, laminar flow occurs when Re is ___ .", "less than about 2000", "The classical laminar limit.", "Below Re ≈ 2000 flow is laminar and parabolic; 2000–4000 is the transition band; above 4000 flow is fully turbulent (for smooth pipes in practice the transition may shift)."),
    P("Assertion (A): The friction factor for laminar pipe flow is 64/Re. Reason (R): In laminar flow viscous forces fully dominate and the velocity profile is parabolic.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Hagen–Poiseuille law gives f = 64/Re.", "Integrating the parabolic profile gives Δp = 32μVL/D², so f = 64/Re exactly. Independent of wall roughness — hence money, Re alone fixes friction."),
    P("Fully turbulent flow in a pipe typically begins at a Reynolds number of about ___ .", "4000", "The common turbulent threshold.", "Re > 4000 → fully turbulent. Turbulent profiles are flatter, with f depending on Re and the relative roughness ε/D, and inertial forces dominate."),
    P("The kinematic viscosity of water at 20°C is approximately ___ .", "1 × 10⁻⁶ m²/s (1 cSt)", "Kinematic value for water.", "ν = μ/ρ = 1.002 × 10⁻³/1000 ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m²/s = 1 cSt. Air at the same temperature has ν ≈ 15 cSt — remarkable because μ_air is small but ρ_air is tiny."),
    P("The hydraulic mean depth of a circular pipe running full is ___ .", "D/4", "Hydraulic radius = area/wetted perimeter.", "R = A/P = (πD²/4)/(πD) = D/4. For a rectangular channel of width b and depth y, R = by/(b + 2y), and R → y for a wide channel."),
    P("Hydraulic radius R is defined as the ratio of ___ .", "the cross-sectional area of flow to the wetted perimeter", "Base definition for channels.", "R = A/P. It is NOT half the actual geometric radius and not the same as hydraulic depth D_h = A/T (depth by top width). These distinctions appear in open-channel exams."),
    P("In fully developed laminar pipe flow the maximum velocity at the axis is ___ times the mean velocity.", "2", "Parabolic profile integral.", "u_max = 2 u_mean for the Poiseuille parabola. In turbulent flow the profile is flatter and u_max ≈ (1.1 to 1.3) u_mean. Hence umean = (umax+... ) changes with regime."),
    P("One centimetre stoke (cSt) equals ___ m²/s.", "10⁻⁶ m²/s", "Stoke to SI kinematic conversion.", "1 St = cm²/s = 10⁻⁴ m²/s; 1 cSt = 10⁻⁶ m²/s. Oil viscosity is quoted in cSt at 40°C and 100°C."),
    P("The Bernoulli equation assumes the flow is ___ .", "steady, incompressible, inviscid and along a streamline", "State the standard assumptions.", "p/ρg + V²/2g + z = constant is valid for steady, frictionless, incompressible flow along a streamline. Real-flow problems add head loss hf and shaft work on the right or left side."),
    P("For compressible steady flow the continuity equation is ___ .", "ρ₁A₁V₁ = ρ₂A₂V₂ = constant", "Mass flow stays constant.", "Mass flow rate m = ρAV stays constant. For incompressible flow ρ is constant, so A₁V₁ = A₂V₂. This is the basis of the venturi and orifice calibrations."),
    P("Assertion (A): The coefficient of discharge of a venturimeter (≈ 0.96–0.98) is higher than that of an orifice meter (≈ 0.62). Reason (R): The venturi has a smooth convergent–divergent cone so energy losses are much smaller.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Venturi recovers pressure in the diffuser.", "The venturi's long diffuser converts kinetic energy back to pressure with little loss; the orifice has a sudden expansion and heavy losses. Hence Cd_venturi ≈ 0.98 while Cd_orifice ≈ 0.62."),
    P("The coefficient of contraction of a sharp-edged orifice is approximately ___ .", "0.64", "Standard cc value.", "cc = Ac/Ao ≈ 0.64 for sharp-edged orifices, cv ≈ 0.98 and cd = cc × cv ≈ 0.62. The classical trio 0.62/0.98/0.64 is the standard memorisation set."),
    P("Torricelli's theorem gives the theoretical velocity of efflux as ___ .", "v = √(2gh)", "Free efflux under gravity head.", "v = √(2gh) results from equating ½ρv² = ρgh. The actual velocity is cv√(2gh); for water through a rounded nozzle cv is near 0.98–1.0."),
    P("A tank has a smooth orifice 6 m below the free surface (cv = 0.98). The actual efflux velocity is ___ .", "≈ 10.6 m/s", "v_actual = cv√(2gh).", "v_ideal = √(2 × 9.81 × 6) = √117.72 ≈ 10.85 m/s; actual = 0.98 × 10.85 ≈ 10.6 m/s. Discharge then uses cd: Q = cd a √(2gh)."),
    P("A horizontal jet of water strikes a fixed vertical plate normally. If the discharge is 0.05 m³/s at 10 m/s, the force on the plate is ___ .", "500 N", "F = ρQV for a stationary plate.", "F = ρQV = 1000 × 0.05 × 10 = 500 N. If the plate moves away at half the jet speed, the relative velocity halves and the force falls to 250 N (F = ρA(V−u)²)."),
    P("A pitot tube measures ___ by converting velocity head into a stagnation rise.", "the local velocity of flow", "Stagnation pressure minus static pressure gives dynamic head.", "Δh = V²/2g, so V = √(2gΔh). Used with a static tap it forms a pitot-static probe; in supersonic flow a compressibility correction applies."),
    P("A rotameter works on the principle of ___ .", "constant pressure drop with variable annular area", "Float balances weight against drag.", "In a rotameter the float settles where the drag equals its weight; the annular flow area varies with height, so the reading scale is nearly linear. It is a variable-area meter measuring local discharge."),
    P("Chezy's formula for open-channel velocity is ___ .", "V = C√(RS)", "Classic mean-velocity formula.", "V = C√(RS), with R the hydraulic radius, S the bed slope and C the Chezy coefficient (units √(m)/s). C = √(8g/f) for pipe analogy."),
    P("Manning's equation for mean velocity in a channel is ___ .", "V = (1/n) R^(2/3) S^(1/2)", "The engineering standard formula.", "V = (1/n)R^(2/3)S^(1/2). For SI, n is dimensionless roughness; for a pipe running full, R = D/4 and the formula is widely used for sewer design."),
    P("The Manning roughness coefficient n for a finished concrete surface is about ___ .", "0.012", "Standard n values memorisation.", "n ≈ 0.012 for finished/fine concrete, 0.013–0.015 for trowelled concrete, ≈ 0.025 for earth, ≈ 0.030–0.035 for gravel/cobbles. Smaller n → smoother, faster flow."),
    P("The Manning roughness coefficient n for an earthen channel in ordinary soil is about ___ .", "0.025", "Soil channel roughness.", "Earth channels n ≈ 0.02–0.03; the representative 0.025 is used in design. Rock channels run 0.035–0.045."),
    P("The Darcy–Weisbach head loss for pipe flow is given by ___ .", "hf = f L V²/(2 g D)", "The universal pipe-loss formula.", "hf = f·(L/D)·V²/(2g). The friction factor f depends on Re and roughness; it is NOT a constant like the old 'friction coefficient' f = 4 for the waterworks loss formula."),
    P("Assertion (A): In laminar pipe flow the Darcy friction factor is independent of wall roughness. Reason (R): The laminar velocity profile burns and the equation f = 64/Re comes only from viscous shear.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Poiseuille flow does not feel roughness.", "For Re below 2000, roughness has no influence; the profile is parabolic and f = 64/Re. Roughness matters only in turbulent flow via ε/D."),
    P("Which one of the following statements about the Moody chart is INCORRECT?", "In the laminar zone the friction factor depends on the relative roughness.", "Laminar f is Re-only.", "The Moody chart has three zones: laminar (f = 64/Re, roughness-free), transition zone, and fully rough turbulent zone where f depends only on ε/D (constant Re lines merge)."),
    P("The equivalent roughness of commercial steel pipe is approximately ___ .", "0.045 mm", "Memorise the roughness of commercial steel.", "ε_steel ≈ 0.045 mm; drawn tubing ≈ 0.0015 mm; cast iron ≈ 0.26 mm; concrete 0.3–3 mm; riveted steel 0.9–9 mm. These feed the relative roughness ε/D."),
    P("The equivalent roughness of concrete pipes ranges roughly from ___ .", "0.3 to 3 mm", "Roughness of concrete work.", "Concrete roughness is large (0.3–3 mm) because of form finishes, joints and aging; PVC/HDPE are far smoother (≈ 0.0015–0.007 mm)."),
    P("The equivalent roughness of a drawn (seamless) tube is about ___ .", "0.0015 mm", "Smooth drawn tubing roughness.", "Drawn tubing ε ≈ 0.0015 mm is the smooth base of the Moody chart. Cast iron sits at ≈ 0.26 mm, about 170× rougher."),
    P("For water at 20°C flowing at 2 m/s in a 100 mm drawn-tube pipe (ν = 1 × 10⁻⁶ m²/s), the Reynolds number is ___ .", "2 × 10⁵ (turbulent)", "Compute Re = VD/ν.", "Re = 2 × 0.1/1 × 10⁻⁶ = 200,000, well above 4000 — fully turbulent, even though the tube is hydraulically smooth."),
    P("For a rectangular channel the critical depth is yc = (q²/g)^(1/3). For a specific discharge q = 2 m³/s per metre width, yc is ___ .", "≈ 0.74 m", "Evaluate (4/9.81)^(1/3).", "yc = (q²/g)^(1/3) = (4/9.81)^(1/3) = (0.4077)^(1/3) = 0.742 m. The specific energy at critical depth is Emin = 1.5 yc ≈ 1.11 m."),
    P("The specific energy E of open-channel flow is ___ .", "E = y + V²/2g", "Sum of depth and velocity head.", "E = y + V²/(2g). For a given discharge, E has a minimum at the critical depth; subcritical flow uses y large/V small, supercritical the reverse. Froude number splits the two regimes."),
    P("The Froude number for open-channel flow is defined as ___ ; flow is subcritical when Fr ___ 1.", "Fr = V/√(gy); Fr < 1", "Define the dimensionless regime number.", "Fr = V/√(gy). Fr < 1 subcritical (tranquil), Fr = 1 critical, Fr > 1 supercritical (rapid). The Froude law governs model–prototype scaling for free-surface flows."),
    P("Assertion (A): A hydraulic jump dissipates excess kinetic energy below a spillway. Reason (R): The jump converts supercritical flow into subcritical flow through turbulence.", "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Energy is destroyed by the jump.", "The jump from y1 to y2 with y2/y1 = ½(√(1 + 8Fr₁²) − 1) has large turbulence losses. Stilling basins use this to protect the river bed; jumps also improve mixing in channels."),
    P("Which one of the following uses of the hydraulic jump is CORRECT?", "It is used as an energy dissipator, a mixer and an aeration device.", "Multiple fell roles of the jump.", "Beyond energy dissipation below spillways, jumps mix chemicals, aerate flow, raise water level for irrigation downstream, and are studied in stilling basins and drop structures."),
    P("For a rectangular weir the discharge is Q = Cd·(2/3)·b·√(2g)·H^(3/2). For b = 1 m, Cd = 0.6 and H = 0.25 m, Q is ___ .", "≈ 0.22 m³/s", "Evaluate the rectangular-weir equation.", "H^1.5 = 0.125; √(2g) = 4.429; Q = 0.6 × (2/3) × 1 × 4.429 × 0.125 = 0.221 m³/s. The head H is measured above the weir crest."),
    P("A 90° V-notch (triangular weir) with Cd = 0.6 and head H = 0.3 m discharges about ___ .", "0.07 m³/s", "Use Q = (8/15)Cd·tan(θ/2)·√(2g)·H^(5/2).", "H^2.5 = 0.0493; tan45° = 1; Q = (8/15)(0.6)(1)(4.429)(0.0493) ≈ 0.0699 m³/s ≈ 70 L/s. Triangular weirs are preferred for measuring small discharges."),
    P("A broad-crested weir of width 1 m with head 0.2 m gives a discharge of about ___ .", "0.15 m³/s", "Q = 1.705·L·H^(3/2) for the free-flow formula.", "H^1.5 = 0.0894; Q = 1.705 × 1 × 0.0894 ≈ 0.153 m³/s. The 1.705 coefficient combines (2/3)·Cd·√(2g) for critical flow over the crest."),
    P("Which one of the following weirs is best suited for measuring small discharges with good sensitivity?", "The V-notch (triangular weir)", "Triangle geometry gives fine resolution at low heads.", "Because Q ∝ H^5/2 for a V-notch, a small head change still produces a measurable discharge change. Rectangular weirs read poorly at very low flows; broad-crested ones need larger heads."),
    P("Which one of the following quantities is non-dimensional?", "Reynolds number", "Re and Froude number are dimensionless.", "Re = ρVD/μ and Fr = V/√(gL) are dimensionless. Stokes' settling velocity (ms⁻¹) and Manning's n (units) are dimensional — n in SI is effectively (s/m^(1/3)) dimensional but treated as numeric."),
    P("Stokes' settling velocity of a spherical particle is ___ .", "vs = g d²(ρs − ρ)/(18 μ)", "Small-particle settling law.", "vs = gd²(ρs−ρ)/(18μ), valid for laminar (Re_p < 1) settling of spheres. Beyond the Stokes regime the drag coefficient and terminal velocity formulas change."),
    P("In Stokes' law the settling velocity scales as ___ of the particle diameter.", "the square", "vs ∝ d².", "Since vs = gd²Δρ/(18μ), doubling the diameter quadruples the settling velocity. This rule drives sedimentation tank sizing: larger grains settle much faster."),
    P("The units of the dynamic viscosity μ appearing in the Stokes and Darcy formulas are ___ .", "Pa·s (kg/(m·s))", "Consistency of viscosity units.", "μ in Pa·s makes Stokes, Darcy–Weisbach and Hagen–Poiseuille dimensionally homogeneous: vs comes out in m/s and hf in metres."),
    P("Water hammer in pipelines is caused by ___ .", "the sudden closing of valves or rapid flow stoppage, producing a pressure surge", "Joukowsky impulse on the elastic column.", "The kinetic energy of the moving column converts suddenly to strain energy: Δp = ρ a ΔV, with a the wave celerity (≈ 1400 m/s for water in steel pipe). Slow valve closure or air chambers reduce it."),
    P("The bulk modulus K of a fluid is defined as ___ .", "K = −V · (dp/dV) = Δp/(ΔV/V)", "Measure of compressive stiffness.", "K = V(dp/dV) with a minus sign; for water K ≈ 2.2 GPa, so a 1% volume squeeze needs ~22 MPa. Compressibility β = 1/K ≈ 2 × 10⁻⁹ Pa⁻¹."),
    P("Which one of the following statements is CORRECT?", "Water is about 100 times more compressible than steel but still very nearly incompressible in hydraulic machinery.", "Relative compressibility of water vs solids.", "Water's K ≈ 2.2 GPa is ~100× smaller than steel (~200 GPa). In practice water is treated incompressible; compressibility matters for water hammer and hydraulic fluid design."),
    P("Which one of the following statements about the coefficient trio cd, cv, cc of a sharp-edged orifice is CORRECT?", "cd is the product of cv and cc and is about 0.62.", "Relation between the three coefficients.", "cd = cv × cc ≈ 0.98 × 0.64 ≈ 0.627 ≈ 0.62. The order matters in numeric problems: first find vena contracta, then the velocity, then multiply."),
    P("The kinematic viscosity of air at 20°C is approximately ___ .", "1.5 × 10⁻⁵ m²/s (15 cSt)", "Air has a high kinematic viscosity.", "ν_air = μ/ρ ≈ 1.8 × 10⁻⁵/1.2 ≈ 1.5 × 10⁻⁵ m²/s = 15 cSt — about 15 times that of water even though μ_air is tiny, because air density is far smaller."),
    P("Ethylene glycol is added to engine coolant mainly to ___ .", "depress the freezing point and raise the boiling point of the water", "Freezing-point and boiling-point modification.", "A 50/50 water–glycol mix freezes near −37°C and boils above 107°C, protecting the engine while still giving good heat capacity and corrosion inhibition."),
    P("Which one of the following fluids has the highest dynamic viscosity at ordinary temperature?", "Glycerine", "Rank known fluid viscosities.", "Glycerine μ ≈ 1.4 Pa·s is about 1000× that of water (1 × 10⁻³ Pa·s); honey and heavy oils are also far more viscous than water, air and mercury."),
  ];

  if (typeof module !== "undefined" && module.exports) module.exports = SSC_JE_ENC3_TOMFLUIDS;
  if (typeof window !== "undefined") window.SSC_JE_ENC3_TOMFLUIDS = SSC_JE_ENC3_TOMFLUIDS;
})();